\section{Introduction}
Fayu (also Sehudate; ISO 639-3 \texttt{fau}; Glottocode fayu1238) is a Lakes Plain language of Papua, Indonesia, spoken by about 1,400 people, mainly around Foida, and classed as Vulnerable by UNESCO per the sources found. It is not known to be taught in schools. Only academic descriptive material was found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primer, textbook or school programme was found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Page Maitland (2020), \emph{A grammatical description of Fayu: a Western Lakes Plain language of West Papua}, MA thesis, University of Newcastle (Glottolog reference entry; described as a grammar sketch): \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/580998}
\item Wikipedia article on the Fayu language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fayu_language}
\item Clouse (1997) on Lakes Plain classification, via Trans New Guinea database: \url{https://transnewguinea.org/source/clouse-1997?page=47}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry: \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/lang/1762?hl=zh-cn}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
There are no known Fayu-language teaching materials. Any curriculum would start from the Maitland grammar and from work with Fayu speakers; Indonesian-language school materials are the likely medium of instruction and were not searched here.
\end{document}
